# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so anything not excluded here is baked
# into the image. Two reasons that matters:
#
#  1. SECRETS. .env holds a live Azure API key. An image layer is readable
#     by anyone who can pull the image, and the key would persist in the
#     registry even after the file is deleted in a later layer. Keys are
#     passed at run time via --secure-environment-variables instead.
#  2. SIZE. The results and the local Coq build are hundreds of MB and are
#     never needed inside the container.

# --- secrets ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# --- results: mounted at run time, must not be pre-seeded ---
phase2_coq/results/
phase2_fol/results/
analysis/tables/
analysis/figs/

# Condition c2/c4 (Coq and FOL alike) shows a model its OWN earlier direct
# answer, read from
#   phase1_nli_eval/results/<dataset>/<dataset>__<model>__zero-shot__en.json
#
# HISTORY: the previous rule was
#     phase1_nli_eval/results/**
#     !phase1_nli_eval/results/*/*__zero-shot__en.json
# which LOOKED correct but silently excluded every Phase-1 file. Docker's
# context builder will not re-include a file whose parent directory was
# excluded by `**`, so the negation never took effect and all 27 c2 Coq
# cells died with `FileNotFoundError: Phase 1 results not found`.
#
# The whole results tree is ~116 MB. Rather than fight Docker's re-inclusion
# rule (which also needs every intermediate directory re-admitted), just
# ship the tree. The build context is a one-time upload; correctness beats
# saving 90 MB of transfer. .env is excluded above, so no secret ships.
phase1_nli_eval/judge_scores/

# --- local toolchains (linux coqc comes from apt) ---
coq812/
coqc_wrap.sh
opamroot/
.opam*
*.vo
*.vok
*.vos
*.glob

# --- python / vcs / editor cruft ---
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.git/
.gitignore
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store
*.swp
